I 100% agree with prioritizing readability as it doesn't matter how fast incorrect code runs. In this case I don't think it sacrifices readability. This is what I was suggesting:
```c#
        internal static bool TryFormat(float value, Span<char> destination, out int charsWritten)
        {
            ReadOnlySpan<char> valueSpan = default;

            if (!float.IsFinite(value))
            {
                if (float.IsNaN(value))
                    valueSpan = "NaN";
                else
                    valueSpan = float.IsNegative(value) ? "-INF" : "INF";
            }
            else if (IsNegativeZero((double)value))
            {
                valueSpan = "-0";
            }
            else
            {
                return value.TryFormat(destination, out charsWritten, "R", NumberFormatInfo.InvariantInfo);
            }

            charsWritten = valueSpan.Length;
            return valueSpan.TryCopyTo(destination);
        }
```

I don't think that complicates the code and is very easy to read. It reduces the number of if checks for a regular number from 4 down to 2.
